```c
if ((features & NETIF_F_HW_TLS_TX) && !(features & NETIF_F_HW_CSUM)) {
    netdev_dbg(dev, "Dropping TLS TX HW offload feature since no CSUM feature.\n");
    features &= ~NETIF_F_HW_TLS_TX;
}
```
Reason:
The bug occurs because the condition checks if the `NETIF_F_HW_CSUM` feature is not set before allowing the `NETIF_F_HW_TLS_TX` feature. This incorrectly blocks devices that only support `IP_CSUM` and `IPV6_CSUM` from utilizing TLS TX offload, as it enforces a stricter requirement than necessary.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
bool ip_csum = (features & (NETIF_F_IP_CSUM | NETIF_F_IPV6_CSUM)) ==
    (NETIF_F_IP_CSUM | NETIF_F_IPV6_CSUM);
bool hw_csum = features & NETIF_F_HW_CSUM;

if (!ip_csum && !hw_csum) {
    netdev_dbg(dev, "Dropping TLS TX HW offload feature since no CSUM feature.\n");
    features &= ~NETIF_F_HW_TLS_TX;
}
```
Reason:
The fix introduces a check that allows the `NETIF_F_HW_TLS_TX` feature if either `IP_CSUM` or `IPV6_CSUM` is set, rather than requiring `HW_CSUM` to be enabled. This change accommodates devices that can handle checksum calculations for either IPv4 or IPv6, thus correctly allowing TLS TX offload for those devices.

